  "id": "COMPOUND:24562 - PROTEIN:5940",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential pathway where 3-Epi-20-Hydroxyecdysone interacts with Thioredoxin, mitochondrial, which in turn targets Carboxylesterase 2 through multiple intermediates. This relationship is supported by the shared target of Thioredoxin, mitochondrial with several compounds, including Diplotrin A, 5-Hydroxy-2-Methylchromone-7-O-Rutinoside, and Flavipin, all of which have a known interaction with Carboxylesterase 2. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Thioredoxin, mitochondrial as a central mediator in the signaling or metabolic regulation of Carboxylesterase 2. The evidence suggests that 3-Epi-20-Hydroxyecdysone may influence the activity of Carboxylesterase 2 through a series of intermediate targets, potentially via a common mechanism involving redox regulation or enzymatic activity. The presence of multiple overlapping targets reinforces the likelihood of a functional link between 3-Epi-20-Hydroxyecdysone and Carboxylesterase 2, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
